hadiths[36]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15856","Waki narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari, from his father, and Ibn Numayr, from Hisham, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ate food and licked his fingers."
"15857","Waki narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, that a slave girl of Kab used to pasture his sheep at Sal, and a wolf attacked one of her sheep, and the shepherdess caught it and slaughtered it with a flint stone. Kab ibn Malik asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he commanded him to eat it."
"15858","Waki narrated to us: Zamah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by him while he was pressing a man for two uqiyyas, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the man: Like this, meaning, reduce half from him. The man said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the man: Pay him what remains of his right."
"15859","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sad, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, licking his three fingers from food."
"15860","Abu Muawiyah narrated to us: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that a black slave girl of theirs slaughtered a sheep of theirs with a flint stone, and he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that, and he commanded him to eat it."
"15861","Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sad, from Abdullah or Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, Abd al-Rahman said, he doubted, meaning Sufyan, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The parable of the believer is like the tender stalk of crops; the winds set it upright, bending it straight at times and laying it down at others, until his term comes to him. And the parable of the disbeliever is like the firmly rooted pine upon its base; nothing shakes it until its uprooting tears it out, or its uprooting is once, Abd al-Rahman doubted."
"15862","Rawh narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, that Kab ibn Malik, when Allah accepted his repentance, came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Allah saved me only through truthfulness, and part of my repentance to Allah is that I never lie again, and that I strip myself of my wealth as charity for Allah Almighty and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you. He said: Then I keep my share at Khaybar."
"15863","Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, he said: Kab ibn Malik said: I was never in an expedition better provided with mounts and provisions than I was in that expedition. He said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out, I said: I shall prepare tomorrow, then catch up with him. So I set about my preparation, and evening came and I had not finished. I said: I shall set about my preparation tomorrow, and the people are still near, then I shall catch up with them. Evening came and I had not finished. When it was the third day I set about my preparation, and evening came and I had not finished. I said: Far from it; the people have marched three days and I have stayed. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, returned, the people began excusing themselves to him, so I came until I stood before him and said: I was never in an expedition better provided with mounts and provisions than I was in this expedition. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, turned away from me and commanded the people not to speak to us, and our women were commanded to withdraw from us. He said: So one day I climbed over a wall, and there I was with Jabir ibn Abdullah. I said: O Jabir, I adjure you by Allah, did you ever know that I cheated Allah and His Messenger even once? He said: He kept silent toward me and would not speak to me. He said: While I was one day, I heard a man on the pass calling Kab, Kab, until he drew near me and said: Give glad tidings to Kab."
"15864","Hajjaj narrated to us: Layth narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, one of the three who were forgiven, that Kab ibn Malik said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, returned from a journey, he began with the mosque and glorified Allah in it with two rakahs, then he greeted and sat in his place of prayer, and the people would come to him and greet him."
"15865","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived from the expedition of Tabuk in the forenoon and prayed two rakahs in the mosque, and when he came from a journey he would do that."
"15866","Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah informed us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived, meaning from Tabuk, and prayed two rakahs in the mosque, and when he arrived from a journey he would do that."
"15867","Abd al-Razzaq and Ibn Bakr narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to him, from his father Abdullah ibn Kab and from his uncle Ubaydillah ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not return from a journey except by day in the forenoon; when he arrived he began with the mosque and prayed in it two rakahs, then sat in it. And Ibn Bakr said in his hadith: From his father Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his uncle."
"15868","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, he said: Umm Mubashshir said to Kab ibn Malik while he was ill: Convey salam to my son, meaning Mubashshir. He said: May Allah forgive you, O Umm Mubashshir, did you not hear what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the Muslim is but a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, returns it to its body on the Day of Resurrection. She said: You spoke the truth; I seek Allah's forgiveness."
"15869","Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: my father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab narrated to me that it reached him that Kab ibn Malik said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the believer when he dies is a bird feeding on the trees of Paradise until Allah, Blessed and Exalted, returns it to its body on the day Allah resurrects him."
"15870","Muhammad ibn Idris, meaning al-Shafii, narrated to us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, that he informed him that his father Kab ibn Malik used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the believer is but a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah, Blessed and Exalted, returns it to its body on the day He resurrects him."
"15871","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out on Thursday in the expedition of Tabuk."
"15872","Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the Muslim is but a bird feeding on the trees of Paradise until Allah, Blessed and Exalted, returns it to its body on the day He resurrects him."
"15873","Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, that Kab ibn Malik said: Seldom would the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, go out when he intended a journey except on Thursday."
"15874","Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, that Kab ibn Malik said: Seldom would the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, go out when he intended a journey except on Thursday."
"15875","Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to me, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, from Kab ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The people will be raised on the Day of Resurrection, and I and my nation will be upon a hill, and my Lord, Blessed and Exalted, will clothe me in a green garment. Then permission will be granted me, and I shall say what Allah wills me to say; that is the praised station."
"15876","Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad ibn Zurarah, that Ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to him, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Two hungry wolves sent among sheep are no more corrupting to them than a man's greed for wealth and honor is to his religion."
"15877","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to me that Kab ibn Malik, when Allah, Blessed and Exalted, revealed about poetry what He revealed, came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has revealed about poetry what You know; what do You see in it? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The believer strives with his sword and his tongue."
"15878","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham informed us that Marwan ibn al-Hakam informed him that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth informed him that Ubayy ibn Kab al-Ansari informed him that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Some poetry is wisdom."
"15879","And Bashir ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab used to narrate that Kab ibn Malik used to narrate that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it is as if you spray them with arrows in what you say to them of poetry."
"15880","Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik informed us that Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari, and he is one of the three who were forgiven, used to narrate that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the believer is but a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah, Blessed and Exalted, returns it to its body on the day He resurrects him."
"15881","Amir ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari, from his father, that he said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when Allah, Blessed and Exalted, accepted his repentance: O Messenger of Allah, I strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you."
"15882","Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abdullah, nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us, from his uncle Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me that Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, and he was the guide of Kab among his sons when he became blind, said: I heard Kab ibn Malik narrating his account when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the expedition of Tabuk. Kab ibn Malik said: I never stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in any expedition but this one except in the expedition of Tabuk, except that I had stayed behind in the expedition of Badr, and he did not reproach anyone who stayed behind from it; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had only gone out seeking the caravan of Quraysh until Allah gathered them and their enemy without appointment. I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the night of Aqaba when we pledged upon Islam; I would not love to have in exchange for it the witnessing of Badr, though Badr is more remembered among the people and more famous. Part of my account when I stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the expedition of Tabuk is that I was never stronger nor better provided than when I stayed behind from him in that expedition; by Allah, I never gathered two mounts before it until I gathered them in that expedition. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whenever he intended an expedition he fought, would misdirect with other than it, until that expedition; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fought it in severe heat, facing a distant journey and desert, and facing many enemies, so he made the matter clear to the Muslims to prepare the preparation of their enemy, and informed them of the direction he intended, and the Muslims with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, were many; no preserving book gathered them, meaning the register. Kab said: Few were the men who intended to absent themselves but thought that would be hidden for him unless revelation from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, came down about it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fought that expedition when fruits were ripe and shade was pleasant, and I was inclined to them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the believers with him prepared for it, and I would go in the morning to prepare with him, then return having accomplished nothing, saying to myself: I am able to do that when I wish. That kept on with me until the people exerted themselves earnestly; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, left in the morning with the Muslims with him, and I had accomplished nothing of my preparation. I said: Preparation after a day or two, then I shall catch up with them. I went in the morning after they had departed to prepare, then returned having accomplished nothing of my preparation. Then I went in the morning and returned having accomplished nothing. That kept on with me until they hastened and the expedition passed beyond. I thought to depart and catch up with them; would that I had done it, then it was not decreed for me. When I went out among the people after the departure of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and roamed among them, it grieved me that I saw none but a man accused of hypocrisy or a man whom Allah had excused. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not mention me until he reached Tabuk. He said, while sitting among the people at Tabuk: What did Kab ibn Malik do. A man of Banu Salimah said: His two cloaks and looking at his sides detained him, O Messenger of Allah. Muadh ibn Jabal said to him: Evil is what you said; by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we know of him only good. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kept silent. Kab ibn Malik said: When it reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had headed back from Tabuk, my grief came to me; I began pondering lies, saying: With what shall I escape his anger tomorrow; seeking help for that from every judicious one of my family. When it was said that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was approaching, falsehood left me, and I knew I would never be saved from him by anything ever, so I resolved upon his truthfulness. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived in the morning, and when he arrived from a journey he began with the mosque, bowed in it two rakahs, then sat for the people. When he did that, the absentees came to him, excusing themselves to him and swearing to him, and they were some eighty men. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, accepted their outward conduct from them, sought forgiveness for them, and left their secrets to Allah, Blessed and Exalted, until I came. When I greeted him he smiled the smile of one angry, then said to me: Come. So I came walking until I sat before him. He said to me: What kept you back; had you not secured your mount. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, had I sat before another of the people of this world, I would have thought I could escape his anger with an excuse; I have been given skill in argument. But by Allah, I knew that if I told you today a lying account with which you are pleased with me, Allah Almighty would soon anger you against me; and if I told you today truthfully, angering you in it, I hope for the comfort of my eye, pardon from Allah, Blessed and Exalted. By Allah, I had no excuse; by Allah, I was never freer nor better provided than when I stayed behind from you. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: As for this, he has spoken the truth; rise until Allah Almighty decides concerning you. So I rose, and men of Banu Salimah hastened and followed me. They said to me: By Allah, we never knew you had sinned a sin before this, and you failed to excuse yourself to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, as the absentees excused themselves; the seeking of forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for you would have sufficed you for your sin. He said: By Allah, they kept blaming me until I wanted to return and belie myself. He said: Then I said to them: Did anyone meet this with me. They said: Yes, two men met it with you; they said what you said, and the like of what was said to you was said to them. He said: I said to them: Who are they. They said: Murarah ibn al-Rabi al-Amiri and Hilal ibn Umayyah al-Waqifi. He said: They mentioned to me two righteous men who had witnessed Badr, in whom I have an example. He said: So I proceeded when they mentioned them to me. He said: And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the Muslims from speaking to us, we three apart from those who stayed behind from him. The people shunned us, he said, and changed toward us until the earth I knew denied itself to me; it was not the earth I knew. We stayed upon that fifty nights. As for my two companions, they submitted and sat in their houses weeping. As for me, I was the youngest of the people and strongest of them; I would attend prayer with the Muslims and roam the markets, and none spoke to me. I would come to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in his gathering after prayer, and greet him, saying to myself: Did he move his lips returning the salam or not. Then I would pray near him and steal glances at him; when I turned to my prayer he looked at me, and when I turned toward him he turned away, until when that of the boycott of the Muslims grew long upon me, I walked until I climbed over the wall of Abu Qatadah, and he is my cousin and dearest of people to me. I greeted him, and by Allah he did not return the salam to me. I said to him: O Abu Qatadah, I adjure you by Allah, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger. He said: He kept silent. He said: I repeated, adjuring him, and he kept silent. I repeated, adjuring him, and he said: Allah and His Messenger know. My eyes overflowed and I turned until I climbed over the wall. While I was walking in the market of Madinah, a Nabatean of the Nabateans of Syria who had come with food selling it in Madinah was saying: Who guides me to Kab ibn Malik. He said: The people began pointing to me for him until he came and handed me a letter from the king of Ghassan, and I was a scribe; in it was: As for after, we have heard that your companion has shunned you, and Allah did not make you in a house of humiliation nor waste; join us, we shall console you. He said: I said when I read it: This too is of the trial. He said: I headed with it to the oven and burned it with it, until when forty nights of the fifty had passed, a messenger of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to me. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commands you to withdraw from your wife. He said: I said: Shall I divorce her, or what shall I do. He said: Rather withdraw from her and do not approach her. He said: And he sent to my two companions with the like of that. He said: I said to my wife: Join your family and stay with them until Allah decides in this matter. He said: The wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, Hilal is an old man, wasted, with no servant; do you dislike my serving him. He said: No, but let him not approach you. She said: By Allah, there is no movement in him to anything; by Allah, he keeps weeping since your matter was what it was until today. He said: One of my family said to me: Had you asked permission of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning your wife; he permitted the wife of Hilal ibn Umayyah to serve him. He said: I said: By Allah, I shall not ask permission concerning her from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I do not know what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would say if I asked his permission, while I am a young man. He said: We stayed after that ten nights, the completion of fifty nights since speech to us was forbidden. He said: Then I prayed the dawn prayer on the morning of fifty nights on the roof of one of our houses. While I was sitting in the state Allah, Blessed and Exalted, mentioned of us, my soul straitened upon me and the earth straitened upon me despite its vastness, I heard a crier standing upon Mount Sal saying with the loudest voice: O Kab ibn Malik, receive glad tidings. He said: So I fell prostrate and knew that relief had come. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, announced to the people the repentance of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, upon us when he prayed the dawn prayer. The people went giving us tidings, and some went toward my two companions giving tidings. A man raced to me on a horse, and a runner of Aslam ran and ascended the mountain, and the voice was faster than the horse. When he whose voice I heard giving me tidings came to me, I stripped for him my two garments and clothed him with them for his tidings; by Allah, I owned nothing else that day, so I borrowed two garments and wore them. I set out heading to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; the people met me throng after throng, congratulating me with repentance, saying: May the repentance of Allah delight you, until I entered the mosque; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was sitting in the mosque with people around him. Talhah ibn Ubaydillah rose to me hurrying until he shook my hand and congratulated me; by Allah, no man of the Muhajirun rose to me but him. He said: Kab would not forget it for Talhah. Kab said: When I greeted the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said, his face shining with joy: Receive glad tidings of the best day that passed over you since your mother bore you. He said: I said: From you, O Messenger of Allah, or from Allah. He said: No, rather from Allah. He said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was delighted, his face lit up as if a piece of the moon, until that was known of him. He said: When I sat before him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is to strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah Almighty and to His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Keep part of your wealth, for it is better for you. He said: I said: I keep my share at Khaybar. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah Almighty saved me only through truthfulness, and part of my repentance is never to speak but truth as I remain. He said: By Allah, I know no one among the Muslims whom Allah blessed in truthful speech since I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, better than Allah, Blessed and Exalted, blessed me. By Allah, I never deliberately lied since I said that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until today, and I hope He preserves me in what remains. He said: And Allah, Blessed and Exalted, revealed: Allah has accepted the repentance of the Prophet and the Muhajirun and the Ansar who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of a party of them almost deviated, then He accepted their repentance; He is to them Kind, Merciful. And upon the three who were left behind, until when the earth straitened upon them despite its vastness, and their souls straitened upon them, and they thought there is no refuge from Allah except to Him, then He accepted their repentance that they repent; Allah is the Accepting, Merciful. O you who believe, fear Allah and be with the truthful. Kab said: By Allah, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, never bestowed upon me any blessing after guiding me greater in myself than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that day, that I did not lie to him, else I would have perished as those who lied to him perished when they lied to him, for Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said of those who lied to him when they lied to him the worst said to anyone. Allah Almighty said: They will swear by Allah to you when you return to them that you turn away from them; so turn away from them; they are filth and their abode is Hell, recompense for what they earned. They swear to you that you be pleased with them; if you are pleased with them, Allah is not pleased with the defiantly disobedient people. He said: And we three were left behind from the matter of those from whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, accepted when they swore; he pledged them and sought forgiveness for them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, deferred our matter until Allah Almighty decided. Thus Allah Almighty said: And upon the three who were left behind. And our being left behind and his deferring our matter which He mentioned is not by our staying behind from the expedition; it is from those who swore to him and excused themselves to him, and he accepted from them."
"15883","Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me that Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik, and he was the guide of Kab among his sons when he became blind, said: I heard Kab ibn Malik narrating his account when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the expedition of Tabuk. Kab ibn Malik said: I never stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in any expedition he fought except in the expedition of Tabuk, except that I had stayed behind from the expedition of Badr, and he did not reproach anyone who stayed behind from it, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had only gone out seeking the caravan of Quraysh in which was Abu Sufyan ibn Harb and a party of Quraysh. Then he said: Come. So I came walking until I sat before him. He said: What kept you back? Had you not purchased your mount? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, had I sat before anyone else of the people of this world, I would have thought I could escape his anger with an excuse, and I have been given skill in argument. And he mentioned the hadith, and he said in it: I hope for the pardon of Allah. And he said: So I said to my wife: Join your family and stay with them until Allah decides in this matter. And he said: I heard the voice of a crier standing upon the highest point of Mount Sal with the loudest voice: O Kab ibn Malik, receive glad tidings. He said: So I fell prostrate and knew that relief had come. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, announced to the people our repentance when he prayed the dawn prayer. And he mentioned the meaning of the hadith of the nephew of Ibn Shihab, and he said in it: And I say to myself: Did he move his lips returning the salam."
"15884","Hasan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-Araj narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik, that he was owed money by Abdullah Abu Hadrad al-Aslami. He met him and clung to him until voices were raised. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by them and said: O Kab, and he gestured with his hand as if saying half. So he took half of what was owed him and left half."
"15885","Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Abbas narrated to us: Abu Uways narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah al-Ansari informed me that Kab ibn Malik used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The soul of the believer is but a bird feeding in the trees of Paradise until Allah Almighty returns it to its body on the day He resurrects him."
"15886","Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father Kab ibn Malik, that he narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent him and Aws ibn al-Hadathan during the days of Tashriq, and they announced that none enters Paradise but a believer, and the days of Tashriq are days of eating and drinking."
"15887","Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah informed us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad ibn Zurarah, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Two hungry wolves sent among sheep are no more corrupting to them than a man's greed for wealth and honor is to his religion."
"15888","Attab ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah informed us, he said: Ibn Luhayah informed us, he said: Musa ibn Jubayr, the freed slave of Banu Salimah, narrated to me that he heard Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrating from his father, he said: The people in Ramadan, when a man fasted and evening came and he slept, food and drink and women became forbidden to him until he broke fast the next day. Umar ibn al-Khattab returned from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, one night after staying late with him, and found his wife had slept. He desired her, and she said: I have slept. He said: You have not slept. Then he had intercourse with her. And Kab ibn Malik did the like of that. Umar went in the morning to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and informed him, so Allah Almighty revealed: Allah knew that you used to deceive yourselves, so He accepted your repentance and pardoned you."
"15889","Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah, nephew of Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Lampoon with poetry; the believer strives with his self and his wealth. By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, it is as if you spray them with arrows."
"15890","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mashar narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm entered upon Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawban and said: O Abu Hafs, narrate to us a hadith from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in which there is no disagreement. He said: Kab ibn Malik narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he immerses in it. And you have immersed, Allah willing, in mercy."
"15891","Yaqub narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Mabad ibn Kab ibn Malik ibn Abi Kab ibn al-Qayn, brother of Banu Salimah, narrated to me that his brother Ubaydillah ibn Kab, and he was among the most learned of the Ansar, narrated to him that his father Kab ibn Malik, and Kab was among those who witnessed Aqaba and pledged to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, there, said: We went out among the pilgrims of our people of the polytheists, and we had prayed and understood, and with us was al-Bara ibn Marur, our elder and our chief. When we headed for our journey and left Madinah, al-Bara said to us: O people, by Allah, I have seen a view, and by Allah, I do not know whether you agree with me upon it or not. He said: We said to him: What is it. He said: I have seen not to leave this building behind my back, meaning the Kabah, and to pray toward it. He said: We said: By Allah, it has not reached us that our Prophet prays except toward Syria, and we do not wish to oppose him. He said: I shall pray toward it. He said: We said to him: But we shall not do. When prayer came we prayed toward Syria and he prayed toward the Kabah until we arrived at Makkah. My brother said, and we had blamed him for what he did, and he refused but to persist upon it. When we arrived at Makkah he said: O nephew, go to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and ask him about what I did in this journey of mine, for by Allah something of it has entered myself when I saw your opposing me in it. He said: So we went out asking about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we did not know him; we had not seen him before that. A man of the people of Makkah met us, and we asked him about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Do you know him. He said: We said: No. He said: Do you know al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, his uncle. We said: Yes. He said: We knew al-Abbas; he would come to us trading. He said: When you enter the mosque, he is the man sitting with al-Abbas. He said: So we entered the mosque, and al-Abbas was sitting and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sitting with him. We greeted, then sat to him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to al-Abbas: Do you know these two men, O Abu al-Fadl. He said: Yes, this is al-Bara ibn Marur, chief of his people, and this is Kab ibn Malik. He said: By Allah, I do not forget the saying of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: The poet. He said: Yes. He said: Al-Bara ibn Marur said: O Prophet of Allah, I went out in this journey of mine and Allah guided me to Islam, and I saw not to make this building behind my back, so I prayed toward it, and my companions opposed me in that until something of that entered myself; what do you see, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You were upon a qiblah, had you been patient upon it. He said: Al-Bara returned to the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and prayed with us toward Syria. He said: His family claim that he prayed toward the Kabah until he died, and that is not as they said; we know better of him than them. He said: And we went out to Hajj, and we promised the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at Aqaba in the middle of the days of Tashriq. When we finished Hajj, and it was the night we had promised the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and with us was Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Haram, Abu Jabir, a chief of our chiefs, and we concealed our affair from those with us of our people of the polytheists, we spoke to him and said to him: O Abu Jabir, you are a chief of our chiefs and noble of our nobles, and we desire for you to leave what you are in, lest you be fuel for the Fire tomorrow. Then we called him to Islam and informed him of the appointment of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he embraced Islam and witnessed Aqaba with us, and he was a naqib. He said: We slept that night with our people in our lodgings until when a third of the night had passed we went out from our lodgings to the appointment of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, slipping secretly like the slipping of sandgrouse, until we gathered in the pass at Aqaba, seventy men, and with us two women of their women: Nasibah bint Kab, Umm Umarah, one of the women of Banu Mazin ibn al-Najjar, and Asma bint Amr ibn Adi ibn Thabit, one of the women of Banu Salimah, and she is Umm Mani. He said: We gathered at the pass awaiting the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until he came to us, and with him that day was his uncle al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and he that day was upon the religion of his people, except that he loved to attend the affair of his nephew and secure for him. When we sat, al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib was the first speaker. He said: O assembly of Khazraj. He said: The Arabs used to call this tribe of the Ansar Khazraj, its Aws and its Khazraj. Muhammad is of us where you know, and we have protected him from our people who are upon the like of our view of him, and he is in might of his people and protection in his land. He said: We said: We heard what you said; speak, O Messenger of Allah, and take for yourself and your Lord what you love. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spoke, recited, called to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and incited to Islam. He said: I pledge you upon that you protect me as you protect your women and your sons. He said: Al-Bara ibn Marur took his hand, then said: Yes, by Him who sent you with the truth, we shall protect you as we protect our waist-wrappers. Pledge us, O Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for we are people of wars and people of arms, inherited elder from elder. He said: The speech was interrupted, while al-Bara was speaking to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, by Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan, ally of Banu Abd al-Ashhal. He said: O Messenger of Allah, between us and the men are ropes, and we are cutting them, meaning the covenants; will it be, if we do that then Allah grants you victory, that you return to your people and leave us. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, smiled, then said: Rather blood is blood and destruction is destruction; I am of you and you are of me; I wage war whom you wage and make peace whom you make peace. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Bring out to me of you twelve naqibs to be over their people. They brought out of them twelve naqibs, nine of Khazraj and three of Aws. As for Mabad ibn Kab, he narrated to me in his hadith from his brother from his father Kab ibn Malik, he said: The first who struck upon the hand of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was al-Bara ibn Marur, then the people followed. When we pledged the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Satan screamed from the head of Aqaba with the farthest voice I ever heard: O people of the dwellings, and al-Jubajib are the dwellings, do you want Mudhamam and the converts with him; they have gathered upon your war. Ali, meaning Ibn Ishaq, says what the enemy of Allah Muhammad says. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This is the Azabb of Aqaba, this is Ibn Uzyab; listen, O enemy of Allah, by Allah, I shall attend to you. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Rise to your lodgings. He said: Al-Abbas ibn Ubadah ibn Nadlah said to him: By Him who sent you with the truth, had you wished we would incline upon the people of Mina tomorrow with our swords. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I was not commanded with that. He said: We returned and slept until morning. When morning came, the chiefs of Quraysh came to us until they came to us in our dwellings. They said: O assembly of Khazraj, it has reached us that you came to this companion of ours to extract him from among us and pledge him upon our war; by Allah, no Arabs are more hateful to us that war break out between us and them than you. He said: Those there of the polytheists of our people rose, swearing to them by Allah that nothing of this was, and we did not know it. They spoke truth; they did not know what was of us. He said: Some of us looked to some. He said: The people rose, and among them was al-Harith ibn Hisham ibn al-Mughirah al-Makhzumi, upon him new sandals. He said: I said a word as if I wished to implicate the people in what they said: You cannot, O Abu Jabir, while you are a chief of our chiefs, to take two sandals like the sandals of this youth of Quraysh. Al-Harith heard it, took them off, then threw them to me. He said: By Allah, you shall wear them. He said: Abu Jabir says: You remembered, by Allah, the youth; return to him his sandals. He said: I said: By Allah, I shall not return them. By Allah, a truce; by Allah, if the omen proves true, I shall strip him of them. This is the hadith of Kab ibn Malik about Aqaba and what he attended of it."
